    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "False. \n\nFor a \\(T\\)-step random walk built from standardized random variables (where each independent increment has a mean of 0 and a variance of 1), the expected mean of the total random walk is exactly 0 and the variance is exactly \\(T\\) [1, 2]. These moments are calculated using the linearity of the expectation operator and hold true for any finite number of steps \\(T\\). They are completely independent of the specific step-level distribution, meaning the results are identical whether the increments are continuous (like a Gaussian distribution) or discrete (like a simple coin toss) [2].\n\nWould you like to move on to how we add scaling parameters to this simple random walk to model real-world asset price volatility and drift?",
