      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The correct answer is **False**. \n\nThe mean and variance of a \\(T\\)-step random walk do *not* depend on the exact choice of distribution for the standard random variable \\(z_t\\). As long as the individual increments are independent and identically distributed with a mean of 0 and a variance of 1, the overall random walk will always have a mean of 0 and a variance that grows linearly with \\(T\\) [1, 2]. This universality is a key feature of the random walk model [3, 4].\n\nWe can move on to the generalized random walk next, where we introduce drift and volatility, if you are ready.",
