    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To model real-world asset price dynamics, we generalize the basic random walk by introducing two constant parameters to each independent step [1]:\n\n*   **\\(\\sigma\\) (Volatility):** A scaling factor multiplied by the standard random variable \\(z_t\\) to measure risk [1].\n*   **\\(\\mu\\) (Drift):** A constant additive piece that models the mean return [1].\n\nCombining these gives us the generalized increment:\n\\[r_t = \\mu + \\sigma z_t\\] [2, 3]\n\nWhen applied to stock prices, \\(r_t\\) typically represents the continuously compounded (logarithmic) return, defined as \\(r_t \\equiv \\log(P_t/P_{t-1})\\) [1, 4]. Because the expectation operator is linear, the total mean and variance over a \\(T\\)-step period simply scale linearly with time, becoming \\(T\\mu\\) and \\(T\\sigma^2\\) respectively [3, 5, 6].\n\nWould you like to look at how we can use this foundation to build more complex processes with time-correlation, like the AR(1) mean-reverting model?",
